{"language": "en", "duration": 93.24, "text": "You pay $40 a month for a gym. You haven't been in six months, and you don't cancel. Why? Because your brain says, I've already paid. This trap is called the sunk cost fallacy. A sunk cost is money, time, or effort already spent that you won't get back, whatever you decide. For economists, a good decision looks only at the future. What's gone is gone. But the brain hates the idea of losing. Stopping means admitting the money is lost, so we keep going to feel like we're not wasting it. In the 1980s, researchers Hal Arks and Catherine Bloomer observed this with theater tickets. People who paid full price attended more performances than people who got a discount. Same play, same enjoyment, but a heavier past expense. You live it every day, finishing a meal you don't like because it's paid for, watching a boring movie to the end, keeping a useless subscription, continuing a project that isn't working. The more you've invested, the harder it is to stop. So how do you get out? First, ask the right question. If I hadn't paid anything yet, would I do this today? If the answer is no, stop. Then count the future cost. Each extra month is $40 more. The past is gone, but the future, you can still save. Every month is a new decision. Finally, turn the loss into a lesson. What you've already paid is the price of learning it didn't suit you. And you, which subscription are you paying for without using it? Tell us in the comments.", "words": [{"word": "You", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.4}, {"word": "pay", "start": 0.4, "end": 0.64}, {"word": "forty", "start": 0.64, "end": 0.93}, {"word": "dollars", "start": 0.93, "end": 1.22}, {"word": "a", "start": 1.22, "end": 1.7}, {"word": "month", "start": 1.7, "end": 1.88}, {"word": "for", "start": 1.88, "end": 2.1}, {"word": "a", "start": 2.1, "end": 2.24}, {"word": "gym.", "start": 2.24, "end": 2.44}, {"word": "You", "start": 2.92, "end": 3.0}, {"word": "haven't", "start": 3.0, "end": 3.28}, {"word"